{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>And this shall be for you, etc.</b> This verse turns back to the priests, to establish for all generations the commandment stated at the beginning of the portion, which is the service of the High Priest — lest one think that it was said only for that one time. Scripture therefore says: <b>this shall be for you as an everlasting statute</b>. <b>To make atonement upon the children of Israel</b> — here HaShem revealed His secret, that He is speaking about the priests, for they are the ones who make atonement." "It further intends thereby that a person should not say that HaShem did not command that he not enter in golden garments except at that time, the first time — when the imprint of the sin of the Golden Calf had not yet been atoned for — but from the first time onward its imprint was already wiped away and erased, and Aaron would come and go into the Holy of Holies in garments of glory. Scripture therefore says: <b>and this shall be for you as an everlasting statute</b>, without change, and concludes: <b>to make atonement upon the children of Israel</b>. Perhaps it intends to give a reason for the matter, saying that even though in the aspect of Aaron himself no imprint of the Calf remained, since in relation to Israel there is still a blemish before them — the imprint of the Calf was not erased at all; and from this standpoint the matter affects even Aaron." "<b>And he did as HaShem had commanded, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim) that the verse intends to declare the praise of Aaron — that he did not act for self-aggrandizement or prominence, but did everything to fulfill the commandment of HaShem. And it appears that it also declares the praise of Moses, for HaShem did not speak with Aaron but with Moses, and the Torah testifies that the discourse was not altered at all by the intermediacy of the intermediary. From this you learn that the things spoken from the mouth of Moses underwent no change, even to a hairsbreadth of a sixtieth part. And this is a foundational principle for our holy Torah, which in its entirety proceeds from the mouth of HaShem — may it grant a person eternal life when he studies it in the sweetness of the Most High."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {2 "The final phrase יְחַיֶּה הָאָדָם חַיֵּי עוֹלָם בַּהֲגִיגוֹ בְּנֹעַם עֶלְיוֹן is read as HaShem being the implicit subject who grants eternal life through Torah study; the syntax is somewhat compressed in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:44.822951876Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}